## a b s t r a c t
Herein, a highly efficient Z-scheme $\mathsf{S n S_{2}/M I L{-}88B}$ (Fe) (SnSFe) heterojunction was successfully synthesized to use both as photocatalysts and persulfate (PS) activator for ibuprofen (IBP) degradation. Flower-liked $\mathsf{S}\mathsf{n}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ was uniformly loaded on MIL-88B (Fe), and SnSFe retained the original polyhedral morphology of MIL-88B (Fe). The highest removal of IBP was achieved in the presence of SnSFe with $0.5\%$ $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}(\mathsf{S n S F e0}.5)$ . Characteristic results and density functional theory calculations demonstrated that the enhanced degradation of IBP was due to the difference in Fermi energy levels of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe) leading to electrons transferred from $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ to MIL-88B (Fe), and $\mathtt{S n-0}$ bond was formed in SnSFe. , OH and $\mathsf{O}_{2}^{-}$ were the main active species in SnSFe0.5/PS/visible light system. Z-scheme heterojunction of SnSFe was constructed to propose the degradation mechanism. This research revealed that the synergism of photocatalysis and PS activation using $S\mathrm{nS}_{2}\vert$ Fe-based MOFs composites possessed great potentials in wastewater remediation.
