## 1. Introduction
Pharmaceutical and personal care products (PPCPs) have been described as a distinct and emerging class of environmental contaminants [1]. The disposal of PPCPs is an urgent task due to their unlimited release and hazardous impact on humans and animals even at low concentrations [2]. Ibuprofen (IBP) as one of the widespread PPCPs is widely used in over-the-counter preparations to relieve pain and fever. Owing to the abundant consumption, IBP is widely present in aqueous environment and has been detected in wastewater, surface water, seawater and even ground water [3]. Moreover, previous study reported that IBP may have a serious risk to human health and ecosystems [4]. Therefore, it is an essential task to develop reliable removal technologies to eliminate IBP from water environment. So far, several approaches have been used to treat IBP in the aquatic environments, such as membrane filtration [5], physical adsorption [6], biodegradation [7], chemical oxidation [8] and especially advanced oxidation processes (AOPs) [9].
Among the AOPs, sulfate radical $(S0_{4}^{-}$ ) based AOPs (SR-AOPs) have been proposed as a prospective strategy for the removal of organic pollutants because of the high oxidation potential and long half-life of $S0_{4}^{-}$ [10]. Generally, persulfate (PS) used as the sources of $\S0_{4}^{--}$ which could be generated by the activation of transition metal ions, light, heat, ultrasound, photocatalytic and heterogeneous catalyst [11]. Due to the abundant existence in the crust of Earth and the nontoxic of iron, iron-based catalysts are considered to be promising candidates to activate PS in aqueous solutions [12]. As newly inorganic and organic hybrid porous materials, metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have the advantages of high surface, regular pore size, open channels and abundant catalysis sites [13], and thus show great potentials in applications of catalysis [14], gas storage [15] and separation [16]. Especially, Fe-based MOFs (Fe-MOFs) could activate PS due to the transformation between Fe(III) and Fe(II) [17]. For example, $80\%$ RhB decolorization was achieved within $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ in rod-like MIL-88A(Fe)/PS system [18]. PS was activated by MIL-101(Fe) and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Fe) to degrade bisphenol F [19]. Although many researchers have studied PS activation by Fe-MOFs, it is still necessary to improve the higher activation efficiency of PS. Light is another popular way to activate PS due to its clean and inexhaustible nature. For example, MIL-88B (Fe)/PS/visible light system was constructed to degrade bisphenol A [20]. $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{MoO}_{4})_{3}$ was prepared to degrade micropollutants via PS activation under visible light illumination [21].
Meanwhile, photocatalytic degradation technology, as another prospective AOPs, has the advantages of high efficiency, simple operation, low energy consumption and no secondary pollution, thus it is also an excellent way to remove PPCPs [22]. Fe-MOFs are one kind of widely used photocatalysts [23]. For example, the synthesized MIL-101(Fe) had a strong photodegradation effect on organic phosphorus under violet light [24]. MIL-53(Fe) and spindly MIL-88B (Fe) exhibited excellent photocatalytic activity for the degradation of dye under visible light [25,26]. However, there are still some defects in the application of nanoscale MOFs for water treatment, such as weak light absorption capacity, insufficient stability and fast electron-hole recombination [27]. Modification of MOFs with other materials is considered as an efficient method to improve the photocatalytic activation of MOFs [12,28,29]. Tin disulfide $(\mathsf{S n S}_{2})$ is an important two-dimensional (2D) layered transition metal sulfide material with N-type semiconductor structure. Studies had shown that $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ had attracted much attention due to its non-toxic, chemical stability, strong redox capacity and excellent catalytic performance in photocatalysis [30]. At present, there were also many reports on $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ composite materials, such as Mo doping [31], Cu doping [32], ${\mathsf{W O}}_{3}@{\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{n S}}_{2}$ heterostructure [33]. All these composites showed good photocatalytic activity under visible light. Therefore, $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ was an ideal co-catalyst to improve the visible light catalytic activity of Fe-MOFs.
Inspired by the above research, this study combined flower liked $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ with MIL-88B (Fe) to construct $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}/\mathsf{M I L}{-}88\mathsf{B}$ (Fe) (SnSFe) heterojunctions as PS activator and photocatalysts to degrade IBP in aqueous solutions. The results of microstructure and chemical characterization indicated the successful formation of SnSFe heterojunctions. The interfacial charge transfer process was analyzed by the combination of experiment and theoretical calculation. Energy band mechanism provided important evidence for the construction of Z-type heterojunction. In addition, repeatability tests showed that the MIL-88B (Fe) and $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ composites had good stability and repeatability.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Chemical reagents
The following chemicals were used directly without further purification. Iron chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $99.0\%$ , N, NDimethylformamide (DMF, $99.5\%$ , ethanol ( $\mathrm{\dot{C}_{2}H_{5}O H}$ , $99.0\%$ , acetic acid $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOH}$ , $99.8\%$ , and NaOH $(98.0\%)$ were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. of China. 1,4- Benzoquinone (BQ, $95.0\%$ ), tert-butanol (TBA, $99.5\%$ , methanol (MeOH, $99.5\%$ , sodium persulfate $\mathsf{\Gamma}(\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{8}$ , $99.0\%$ and tin chloride pentahydrate $(\mathrm{SnCl}_{4}{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $98.0\%$ ) were purchased from Titanchem Co., Ltd. of Shanghai. Thioacetamide (TAA, $99.0\%$ (Adamas) and terephthalic acid $_{\mathrm{{H_{2}B D C}}}$ , $99.0\%$ were obtained from Aladdin Chemistry Co., Ltd, Shanghai, China. Deionized water.
## 2.2. Catalysts preparation
## 2.2.1. Preparation of $S n S_{2}$
$\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ was composited through the common solvothermal method with some modification [34]. Briefly, $876.5~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathsf{S n C l}_{4}$ -  $5\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ was dissolved into the mixture solution of ethanol $(33.5~\mathrm{mL}$ and acetic acid $\mathrm{:}1.5\ \mathrm{mL}$ . Then, $469.6~\mathrm{mg}$ of TAA was added into the mixture accompanied by the magnetic stirring. A limpid solution attained, and the solution was introduced into a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave, which was heated at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ After it was cooled down to room temperature, the product was washed several times with deionized water and ethanol. Finally, the product was dried at $80^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ in a vacuum oven.
## 2.2.2. Synthesis of MIL-88B (Fe)
MIL-88B(Fe) was synthesized by hydrothermal method followed the procedures based on the previous studies [35], and the detailed synthesis steps were described in Text S1.
## 2.2.3. Synthesis of SnS2/MIL-88B (Fe)
By adjusting the mass ratio of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe), a series of SnSFe composites were prepared by solvothermal method. The detailed operation was as follows: firstly, an appropriate amount of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ powder was dissolved in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF, followed by ultrasonic treatment for $^\mathrm{~3~h~}$ to make it evenly dispersed. Then, $_{1.66\mathrm{~g~}}$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}$ was dissolved in the solution. Subsequently, $4.7\textrm{g C H}_{3}–$ COOH, $2.7\ g\ F e C l_{3}{\cdot}6\ H_{2}O$ and $0.32\ \mathrm{g\NaOH}$ were added in the above solution in turn, and kept stirring until a uniform solution was formed. Finally, the homogeneous solution was transferred to a Teflon-lined autoclave and the reaction conditions were $110~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The following steps were the same as the synthesis of MIL-88B (Fe). The prepared samples were labeled as SnSFex (SnSFe0.1, SnSFe0.2, SnSFe0.5, SnSFe1.0 represented the mass percentage of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ in the composite material was $0.1\%$ , $0.2\%$ , $0.5\%$ , $1.0\%$ , respectively).
## 2.3. Experimental procedure, characterization, analytical methods and DFT calculation
The characterization methods, experimental procedure, analytical methods and the relevant parameters of density function theory (DFT) calculation are provided in Text S2.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterizations of samples
The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the as-synthesized $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ , MIL-88B (Fe), and SnSFe hybrids are displayed in Fig. 1. It could be seen that the XRD peaks of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ sample matched well with the standard plane position of JCPDS 022-951 [36], indicated that $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ was successfully synthesized. For MIL-88B (Fe), diffraction peaks at 9.4, 12.5, 18.9, and $22.0^{\circ}$ corresponded to the (101), (102), (202) and (211) crystal surfaces of MIL-88B (Fe) were observed, which was consistent with the characteristic diffraction peaks of Simulation and the previous reports [37]. This meant that MIL-88B (Fe) was successfully prepared. The XRD patterns of SnSFe composites exhibited almost the same patterns with MIL-88B (Fe). However, the characteristic peaks of MIL-88B (Fe) in SnSFe hybrids gradually weakened with the increasing doping of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ . Moreover, compared with the pattern of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ , it was found that the characteristic peak of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ at $32.2^{\circ}$ was shifted to $31.7^{\circ}$ in SnSFe, and this peak increased with the increase of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ doping. This phenomenon might be caused by the formation of heterostructure between MIL88B (Fe) and $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ , which enlarged the interlayer distance of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ [38,39]. Thus, the peak at $31.7^{\circ}$ could be ascribed to the loading of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ in the SnSFe hybrids. These results indicated that $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ was successfully loaded on the surface of MIL-88B (Fe), and the heterojunction between $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe) was successfully constructed.
In order to explore the morphology of the synthesized samples, the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images of the $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ , SnSFe0.5 and MIL-88B (Fe) are acquired. As shown in Fig. S2a, the SEM image of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ illustrated a flower-like microsphere structure, which was in line with the previous report [40]. Fig. S2b is the SEM image of MIL-88B (Fe). Obviously, MIL-88B (Fe) displayed a truncated hexagonal cone with particle size about $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , which was consistent with the previous literatures [41,42]. From the SEM image of SnSFe0.5 (Fig. 2a), it was obvious that SnSFe0.5 exhibited a roughly truncated hexagonal cone morphology with particle size about $2.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , which was almost the same as MIL-88B (Fe). Notably, there were granular materials presented on the surface of SnSFe0.5 and regular cracks formed at both ends of truncated hexagonal cone, which attributed to the instruction of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ . The presence of regular cracks illustrated that the doping of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ might have affected the reaction of reactants during the synthesis of the SnSFe0.5 mixture, thus alters the crystallization between the $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ and the organic ligands. The introduction of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ could influence the nucleation and growth process, and finally affect the structure and morphology of the material as mentioned in previous reports [43]. TEM image (Fig. 2b) showed SnSFe0.5 had the same morphology with that exhibited in SEM image. Moreover, it was clearly that $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ adhered to the surface of SnSFe0.5 (Fig. 2c). In addition, high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) analysis further confirmed the microstructures of SnSFe0.5 composite. As shown in Fig. 2d, the interface between MIL-88B (Fe) and $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ was clearly observed, demonstrating $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ was intimately deposited on the surface of MIL-88B(Fe). The energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) results (Fig. 2e and Fig. S3) showed that O, Fe, Sn and S elements existed in SnSFe0.5 and they were evenly distributed in SnSFe0.5 composite. These results were deeper confirmed the successfully combination of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe).
To explore the surface areas and porous structures of SnSFe, $\ensuremath{\mathrm{SnS}}_{2}$ and MIL-88B(Fe), the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption measurements are carried out, and the results are presented in the Table S1 and Fig. 3. As illustrated in Fig. 3a, the isotherm of MIL88B(Fe) was type IV, indicating MIL-88B(Fe) had a distinct mesoporous structure characteristic. The isotherms of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and SnSFe displayed the co-existence of type I and IV $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption curves, implying mesoporous and micropores were both existed in these samples. These results were agreement with the pore size distribution displayed in Fig. 3b. Meanwhile, Table S1 showed that SnSFe0.5 $\scriptstyle(76.7\ m^{2}/\mathbf{g})$ and SnSFe0.2 $(79.3\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ had higher specific surface area than the other samples, implying SnSFe0.5 and SnSFe0.2 could provide more active sites for adsorption and reaction, and thus facilitated the catalytic degradation efficiency [44].
Fig. 4 and Fig. S4 illustrated the Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra of the as-prepared samples. Compared all the FT-IR spectra of the samples, it was obvious that all SnSFe composites shared the similar FT-IR spectrum with MIL-88B (Fe). However, the characteristic peaks of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ were not observed in SnSFe composites, which may be due to the low content of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ . The sharp peak at $554~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was attributed to the symmetrical stretching vibration of the FeAO bond [20], while the peaks at 1663, 1602 and $1383~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were attributed to the vibrations of carboxyl groups of the organic linker [27,45]. Notably, the absorption peak at $554~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ gradually weakened with the increasing loading concentration of $\mathsf{S}\mathsf{n}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ , which may be ascribed to the introduction of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ leading to the break of partial FeAO bond.
The chemical states of Fe, O, C, Sn and S on the surface of SnSFe0.5 hybrid are determined by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopic (XPS). C, O, Fe, Sn and S elements were detected from the full survey spectrum, demonstrating the co-existence of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL88B (Fe) in SnSFe0.5 composite (Fig. 5a). The C 1s spectra of SnSFe0.5 and MIL-88B (Fe) were shown in Fig. 5b. The peak at
$284.8\ \mathrm{~eV}$ could be ascribed to $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ -bonded $c{\mathrm{-}}C$ and $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bands [46]. The peak at $288.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ appointed to the benzoic ring and the carboxylate groups $(0{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}{=}0),$ belonging to the organic ligand of MOFs. Moreover, compared to MIL-88B (Fe), a new peak at
$285.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ of SnSFe0.5 was observed, which could be appointed to $C{\mathrm{-}}0{\mathrm{-}}S{\mathrm{n}}$ . The same phenomenon was found in the composite of $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ and conjugated derivative of polyvinyl alcohol [47]. As depicted in Fig. 5c, the O 1s spectrum of SnSFe0.5 could be manifested into three peaks. Peaks at 532.5, 530.2 and $531.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ corresponded to OAH, FeAO and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ , respectively. Compared with the O 1s spectrum of MIL-88B (Fe), it could be found that the peak of FeAO (530.2 eV) was weaker than that in the spectra of SnSFe0.5. The results of C 1s and O 1s spectra further confirmed the formation of $S\mathrm{n-}0$ in $\mathsf{S n S F e0.5}$ heterostructure, demonstrating the successful synthesis of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe) were combined by $\mathsf{S n{-}}0$ , which were consistent with the FT-IR results. Two main peaks and one satellite peak were observed in Fe 2p spectrum of SnSFe0.5 (Fig. 5d). Peaks at 711.7 and $725.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ were attributed to Fe $\scriptstyle2{\mathrm{p}}3/2$ and Fe $2\mathsf{p}\ 1/2$ of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ , respectively. Peaks at 713.6 and 729.2 eV were assigned to Fe $\scriptstyle2{\mathrm{p}}3/2$ and Fe $2\mathsf{p}\ 1/2$ of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ while the peak at $718\ \mathrm{eV}$ was attributed to the satellite signal [37]. Interestingly, compared with $\mathsf{S n S F e0.5}$ , the Fe 2p peaks (at 711.7, 713.6, and $718{\mathrm{~eV}})$ of MIL-88B (Fe) composite was shifted to the lower binding energy. The slight negative shifting of the Fe $\mathsf{2p}$ peaks indicated an enhanced electron density on $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ [48]. The reason for these phenomena might be the change of electronic state during the formation of heterostructure between MIL-88B (Fe) and $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ . XPS spectrum of $\mathrm{Sn}~3\mathrm{d}$ region (Fig. 5e) contained two individual symmetric peaks at 486.9 and $495.3\mathrm{eV}$ , which were ascribed to Sn disulfide and Sn dioxide, respectively [49]. Fig. 5f showed S 2p XPS spectrum, peaks at 161.8 and 163.3 eV were assigned to $\mathtt{S2p3}/2$ and $\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}\thinspace1/2$ , respectively [39]. In the previous studies, the peaks of Sn and S in pure $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ were in the range of 486.65–486.7 eV (Sn disulfide), 495.0–495.18 eV (Sn dioxide), 161.5–161.74 eV $(\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}3/2)$ and 162.6 eV $(\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}1/2)$ , respectively [50-53]. Compared with $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ in these previous reports, the peaks of Sn and S in SnSFe0.5 were positively shifted, indicating the photoexcited electrons transferred from $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ to MIL-88B(Fe). This result is consistent with the results obtained in the XPS spectrum of Fe 2p. The analysis of XPS further confirmed the presence of SnAO coordination and suggested the electrons was transferred from $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ to MIL-88B(Fe) in SnSFe0.5 hybrids.
## 3.2. Density functional theory calculation
DFT calculation was employed to evaluate interfacial charges with materials studio. The Fermi level of a semiconductor was regarded as an important parameter for evaluating the charge
transfer on the surface of catalysts. The Fermi energy could be gained from equation (1) [54].
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\boldsymbol{\mathcal{O}}=\mathsf{E}_{\nu a}-E_{f}}\end{array}
$$
The calculated electrostatic potentials of MIL-88B (Fe) and $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ are shown in Fig. 6a and b. The calculation work functions of MIL88B (Fe) and $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ were 5.71 and $4.61\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. The results indicated that the Fermi level of MIL-88B (Fe) was lower than that of $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ , indicating charges transfer could be induced from $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ to MIL-88B (Fe) [54], which was consistent with the results of XPS.
The atomic electron density difference diagram and local electron density difference diagram at the interface of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL88B (Fe) heterojunction are shown in Fig. 6c and d, respectively. In Fig. 6c, the differential profiles of the crystal plane of MIL-88B (Fe) and $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ illustrated the plotted contours were between $-0.02963$ and $0.03695~\mathrm{e}/\mathring{\mathrm{A}}.$ Red represented the regions of high charge density and blue indicated the regions of low charge density. It could also be seen that the redder colored regions were close to S and O element, while bluer colored regions on the surface of SnSFe heterojunction were close to Sn element. Furthermore, it was obvious that $S\mathrm{n}{-}0$ was formed, suggesting the electrons were redistributed when the heterojunction was formed. Electron density on the interface of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe) was studied via the local electron density difference diagram (Fig. 6d), which setting Sn and S as one part (SnAO), and Fe, C, O, and H as the other part (MIL-88B (Fe)). It was found that the red and blue regions were close to MIL-88B(Fe) and $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ , respectively, and there were accumulated electrons around O and Fe element. These results deeper confirmed the formation of the heterojunction and the flow of electrons from the surface of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ to MIL-88B (Fe). Ultimately, the electron density of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ decreased, and the electron density of MIL-88B (Fe)increased, which was consistent with the direction of charge transfer revealed by XPS analysis. Meanwhile, an internal electric field (IEF) was produced between $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe), which would greatly favor the transfer of electron-hole pairs between $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe) after photo-excitation.
## 3.3. Catalytic degradation of IBP
The catalytic activity of SnSFe composites is evaluated by the removal of IBP under visible light illumination in the presence of PS, and the results are shown in Fig. 7a. It was obvious that all the catalysts nearly attained the adsorption-desorption equilibriums within $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ , and IBP removal efficiency by the absorption of SnSFe composites was consistent with the specific surface areas of these catalysts. The degradation efficiency of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ for IBP was only $22.0\%$ within $180~\mathrm{{min}}$ in the presence of PS, indicating $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ could not degrade IBP effectively. Compared with MIL-88B (Fe), SnSFe composites exhibited enhanced degradation efficiency of IBP. The degradation efficiency of IBP increased with the increasing $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ doping until the concentration of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ reached $0.5\%$ . Almost $100\%$ of IBP removal efficiency was achieved within $120\ \mathrm{min}$ . It was obvious that SnSFe0.5 displayed the best catalytic activity. Furthermore, the degradation of IBP followed to quasi-first-order kinetics $(\ln(\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{C}_{0})=-k\mathsf{t})$ (Fig. 7b). The kinetic rate constants $(k)$ and correlation coefficients $(\mathsf{R}^{2})$ were listed in Table S2. ${\tt R}^{2}$ showed that the kinetic curves were strongly correlated with the experimental results. The $k$ value of SnSFe0.5 was about 54.0 and 4.4 times higher than that of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe), respectively. For comparison, IBP degradation over visible light alone, PS and visible light, SnSFe0.5 and visible light, and SnSFe0.5 with PS in dark conditions were performed (Fig. S5). It was observed that IBP could not achieve an effective degradation only by PS or photocatalysis. The synergism of PS activation and photocatalysis enhanced the degradation of IBP. Furthermore, the role of visible light was more important than PS activation. Additionally, many catalysts were used to decompose IBP. For example, $99.80\%$ removal of IBP was achieved in FeO modified Iranian clinoptilolite at $210\ \mathrm{min}$ under solar light irradiation [55]. IBP degradation efficiency was $91.8\%$ in the system of $\mathsf{C a C u_{3}T i_{4}O_{12}}$ peroxymonosulfate/visible-light at $60\ \mathrm{min}$ [56]. IBP could be completely degraded within $120\ \mathrm{min}$ in carbon encapsulated $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ grafted on carbon nanofibers activated PS system [57]. Obviously, the degradation rate of IBP in visible-light/SnSFe0.5/PS (Vis/SnSFe0.5/PS) system was comparable with the previous reports and SnSFe heterojunctions were effectively catalysts to degrade IBP with the addition of PS under visible light irradiation. The total organic carbon (TOC) was adopted to analyze the mineralization of IBP over SnSFe0.5 in the presence of PS under visible light irradiation. As shown in Fig. S6, after turning on the light for $180~\mathrm{{min}}$ , the TOC content in the solution was $15\%$ , which indicated that most IBP was finally decomposed into ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ .
## 3.4. Photocatalytic mechanism
To study the main active species in $\mathrm{Vis/SnSFe0.5/PS}$ system, the radical quenching experiments were carried out. TBA, MeOH, and BQ were used as quenching agents of OH, $\mathsf{S O}_{4}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$ , respectively. As depicted in Fig. 7c, the addition of BQ, TBA, and MeOH presented significant inhibition effect on IBP degradation, which demonstrated that $S0_{4}^{--}$ , $\mathbf{O}_{2}^{-}$ , and OH took significant roles during the degradation of IBP. To deeper identify the main active radicals affecting IBP degradation, the electron spin resonance (ESR) experiments using DMPO free radical traps were performed. As shown in Fig. 7d, the characteristic signals of $S0_{4}^{--}$ , $0_{2}^{\cdot-}$ , and OH appeared in the spectrogram, further indicating $S0_{4}^{--}$ , $0_{2}^{\cdot-}$ and OH were generated during the catalytic degradation process.
To investigate the mechanism of the Vis/SnSFe0.5/PS system, UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra (UV–vis DRS) measures are applied to obtain the optical properties of as-prepared materials and the results are shown in Fig. 8a. It could be seen that MIL88B (Fe) and SnSFe all exhibited strong light responses in the range of $200{-}600\mathrm{nm}$ . Compared to MIL-88B (Fe), SnSFe composites had a stronger optical response band between 200 and $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ , indicating SnSFe heterojunctions possessed better visible light absorption capacity than MIL-88B (Fe). Notably, SnSFe0.5 showed highest light adsorption ability between 400 and $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which was beneficial to enhance the photocatalytic efficiency. It agreed with the results of IBP catalytic degradation performances. The band gap energy $(\mathtt{E_{g}})$ of $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ , MIL-88B (Fe), and SnSFe0.5 was determined by the UV–vis DRS. The following Tauc’s equation (Eq. (2)) was used to determine $\mathtt{E_{g}}$ of the prepared catalysts [58].
$$
\left(\alpha h\nu\right)^{n/2}=A(h\nu-E_{g})
$$
Where $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ was the absorption coefficient, hv was the photon energy, A was a constant, $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{g}}$ was the band gap energy, and n was the integer [59]. The values of n could be $1/2$ (Direct semiconductors) or 2 (Indirect semiconductors) [60]. The optical $\mathsf{E}_{\mathrm{g}}$ of MIL-88B (Fe), $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and SnSFe0.5 composite calculated in Fig. 8b were $2.21\ \mathrm{eV}$ $2.25\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $2.15\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, indicated that band gap values of SnSFe0.5 became narrower after the combination of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe). Thus, SnSFe0.5 possessed the smallest band gap and highest light adsorption, which implied that SnSFe0.5 was more prone to electron-hole pairs separation and thus more beneficial for IBP photocatalytic degradation than the other prepared catalysts.
Photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopy and electrochemical experiments were conducted to further evaluate the photogenerated charge separation and migration efficiency. The PL spectra of MIL-88B (Fe) and SnSFe0.5 are exhibited in Fig. 8c. It could be found that the PL emission peak intensity of MIL-88B (Fe) was higher than that of SnSFe0.5, which suggested that the electronhole pairs generated from the surface of SnSFe0.5 possessed a prolonged lifetime. This may be due to the enhanced electron-hole separation efficiency by the SnSFe heterojunction between MIL88B (Fe) and $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ . Furthermore, $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ may be a good electron acceptor in photocatalytic reactions in the SnSFe heterojunction, which could well inhibit the recombination of the photogenerated electron-hole pairs. The result was also in good accordance with the outcomes of photocatalytic performance and DFT.
The photocurrent-time curves of MIL-88B (Fe) and SnSFe0.5 are shown in Fig. 8d, in which SnSFe0.5 exhibited a stronger photocurrent response compared to MIL-88B(Fe). It was confirmed that the introduction of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ was benefit for the separation and transfer of photoexcited charge carriers. Fig. 8e was the experimental electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) spectra of SnSFe0.5 and MIL-88B (Fe). The high frequency semicircle of the Nyquist diagram represented the surface charge transfer impedance of the photocatalyst. The Nyquist arc radius of SnSFe0.5 was significantly smaller than that of MIL-88B (Fe), suggesting that SnSFe0.5 had higher electron migration efficiency. These results all indicated that the formation of heterojunction between $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe) could improve the photoexcited electron migration efficiency.
In order to further understand the reasons for enhanced photodegradation activity of SnSFe0.5 catalyst on IBP, Mott-Schottky analysis was performed on SnSFe0.5, MIL-88B(Fe) and $\mathsf{S}\mathsf{n}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ catalyst in a dark environment of $1\mathrm{\kHz}$ (Fig. 8f and S7). The slope of Fig. 8f and S7 showed that SnSFe0.5, $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe) were N-type semiconductor. According to the Mott-Schottky diagram, the flat band potential of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe) were about $-0.10$ , $-0.45$ and $-0.43\ \mathrm{eV}$ relative to NHE, respectively. The flat band potential of N-type semiconductors was $0.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ higher than its conduction band potential $\left(\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{C B}}\right)$ [12], thus the $\mathtt{E}_{\mathtt{C B}}$ of SnSFe0.5, $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe) were $-0.20$ , $-0.55$ and $-0.53\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. Significantly, since the $S0_{4}^{-}/S_{2}0_{8}^{2-}$ redox potential $(2.0\ \mathrm{eV})$ was higher than the CB potential of SnSFe0.5, the reaction of $S_{2}0_{8}^{2-}$ with photoexcited electrons could produce $S0_{4}^{--}$ . Meanwhile, photoexcited holes reacted with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to form OH. These results were consistent with those of free radical quenching and ESR experiments. But when it came to $\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$ , contrary to the conventional mechanism, the electrons in the CB of SnSFe0.5 was not enough to produce $0_{2}^{\cdot-}$ $(\mathsf{O}_{2}/\mathsf{O}_{2}^{-}=-0.33\ \mathrm{eV})$ , which was contradictory to the ESR experimental results. Therefore, a Z-scheme charge transfer mechanism need to be introduced to generate $\dot{0_{2}^{-}}$ .
Combined with above analysis, the band structure of SnSFe0.5 and the mechanism of IBP degradation in Vis/SnSFe0.5/PS are shown in Fig. 9. Firstly, during the formation of the heterojunction, electrons flowed from the surface of $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ to the surface of MIL-88B (Fe) (Fig. 8a), which made the region of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe) was located a positive and negative charged region, respectively, and a kind of built-in electric field was formed in the positive and negative regions, which was well consistent with the DFT [33]. After the formation of SnSFe0.5 heterojunction, $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and MIL-88B (Fe) were excited to produce photogenerated electron-hole pairs under visible light irradiation (Eq. (3)). At the same time, the electrons in the CB of MIL-88B (Fe) tended to bind to the holes in the VB of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ [60]. And the presence of the built-in electric field accelerated the constant flow electrons from the CB of MIL-88B (Fe) to the VB of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ . This extremely well suppressed the recombination of photogenerated electron-hole pairs. The result was consistent with the results of PL. According to the Mott-Schottky diagram, the CB of $\mathsf{S n S F e0.5}$ was only $-0.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ which was higher than the $0_{2}/0_{2}^{\cdot-}$ redox potential $(-0.33\ \mathrm{\eV})$ , which was contradictory to the ESR results. Therefore, $0_{2}^{\cdot-}$ occurred in the CB of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ in Zscheme $\mathrm{\mathrm{'}S n S_{2}@S n S F e},$ heterojunction (Eq. (4)). $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ and electrons could be used to activate PS to $\S0_{4}^{--}$ (Eq. (6)). The $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ could be transformed into $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ with the irradiation of visible light by Eq. (7), and then generate $\S0_{4}^{--}$ by Eq. (6). $S0_{4}^{2-}$ generated from the Eq. (5) and (6), also reacted with photoexcited holes to generate $\S0_{4}^{--}$ (Eq. (8)), and thus accelerated the formation of $S0_{4}^{\cdot-}$ . In addition, the holes could also promote the formation of OH. Finally, all three radicals and holes could be used for IBP degradation.
