## 3.5. The stability of SnSFe0.5 composite
The stability of SnSFe0.5 photocatalyst was evaluated to assess potential for practical applications of catalyst. To assess the stability of SnSFe0.5 composites, the cycle experiments were carried out. The method to obtain the recycled catalyst was listed in Text S1. As shown in Fig. 10, the removal rate of ibuprofen could still reach $98\%$ after three cycles of test, indicating that the composite material SnSFe0.5 had good stability. By comparing the FT-IR and XRD (Fig. S8) spectra of SnSFe0.5 before and after the reaction, it was found that the FT-IR and XRD spectra of SnSFe0.5 did not change significantly, which further indicated that the SnSFe0.5 had good physical and chemical stability.
## 3.6. Degradation pathway
LC-TOF-MS was used to detect the main degradation byproducts in the Vis/SnSFe0.5/PS system. As shown in Fig. 11, the proposed IBP degradation by-products could be summarized as five products with $m/z$ of 205 (IBP), 221 (P1), 163 (P2), 177 (P3, P4), and the Mass spectra of the products were displayed in Fig. S9. Product 1 with $m/z=221$ was the hydroxylate product of IBP generated by the attack of OH, which was a further demonstration of presence of OH in the Vis/SnSFe0.5/PS system. With further oxidation of product 3 and 4 with $\mathrm{m}/s=177$ were produced. Meanwhile, since $S0_{4}^{--}$ , OH and $0_{2}^{\cdot-}$ could also oxidize IBP, product 2 $(m/$ $z=163$ ) could also be observed. Finally, these products were mineralized into ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ [61].
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, $S{\mathrm{nS}}_{2}/{\mathrm{MIL}}{-}88\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Fe})$ (SnSFe) composites were synthesized by a one-pot hydrothermal method, and used to study the effect of catalytic degradation for IBP under visible light irradiation with PS. The results showed that SnSFe0.5 showed the strongest degradation of ibuprofen with $100\%$ removal rate at $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ , which was 54.0 times higher than that of $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ and 4.4 times higher than that of MIL-88B(Fe). Basic characterization and DFT theory showed that SnSFe is not simply compounded, but may form a Ztype heterojunction through $S\mathrm{n-}0$ bonding. The presence of $0_{2}^{\cdot-}$ in the main active species further supports the formation of a Ztype heterojunction. Theoretical calculations and the plots of the $(\mathsf{a h}\nu)^{1/2}$ against hv photo energy demonstrated that SnSFe heterojunction more favored to promotes electron separation, transfer, and suppression of electron-hole pair recombination, thereby improving the catalytic performance of SnSFe0.5. Consequently, the band gap energy of SnSFe0.5 is lower than that of many heterojunction materials [62-64]. In addition, SnSFe0.5 was found to have a strong light absorption capacity and good physical and chemical stability. This study provided ideas for modification of MIL-88B (Fe) by $S\mathrm{n}S_{2}$ , and the improvement of photocatalytic activity of SnSFe by SR-AOPs. And this material was expected to be a potential material for degrading organic pollution in aqueous solution.
